      "text": "Since the sum of any three consecutive terms is always $30$, we have:\n\\[A+B+C=30,\\qquad B+C+D=30,\\qquad C+D+E=30,\\qquad D+E+F=30,\\]\n\\[E+F+G=30,\\qquad F+G+H=30,\\qquad G+H+A=30\\qquad\\text{(1)}.\\]\nAdding equations $(1)$, we get:\n\\[2(A+B+C+D+E+F+G+H)=180\\Longrightarrow A+B+C+D+E+F+G+H=90.\\]\nNow we have:\n\\[C+F+H=30.\\qquad (2)\\]\nSubstituting this into equation $(1)$, we have:\n\\[A+D+G=60\\qquad (3).\\]\nFrom $(2)$, we have $F=30-C-H$.  Substituting, we get:\n\\[60=A+D+G=5+D+G.\\qquad (4)\\]\nDividing equation $(4)$ by $4$, we have:\n\\[15=\\frac{A}{4}+\\frac{D}{4}+\\frac{G}{4}.\\qquad (5)\\]\nSince $A+B+C=30$ and the sum of any three consecutive terms is always $30$, we have $B=5+G$.  Then Equation $(1)$ implies $A=15+G$.  Equations $(5)$ and $(4)$ imply $G=0$.  $D=E=F=15$.\n\nWe now have $D+E+F=45$.  Therefore, $A=15$, $D=E=F=15$, and $H=15$, so $A+H=30$.  The answer is  \\[\\boxed{30}.\\]",
