      "text": "Let $A=A_1,A_2,A_3,\\ldots,A_n$ and $H=H_1,H_2,H_3,\\ldots,H_n$, where $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\nThen it follows that in the eight-term sequence, $\\begin{cases}A+B=D,\\quadB+C=E,\\quadC+D=F\\\\E+F=G,\\quadG+H=A,\\quadH+A=B\\end{cases}\\quad(*)$ then we combine \\[D+H=G\\quad \\text{and}\\quad H+B=G \\]then we have $B=D$, so the best \\[B=E=H=A=D+D=F+C=D+G\\]\n       \nBy $(1)$-&-&&-(2) coupled with $(4)$-&-&-(5) in \\[(1),(3)\\quad \\quad(4),(6)\\quad \\quad(2),(3)\\quad \\quad(5),(6)\\]So $B=C=E=D=G=F=A=H=5$\n\n$\\therefore A+H=10$.\n\nOR\n\\[(30,C,D)=\\text{def}A,B,C,D\\leftarrow(A+B=D,C+B=E,D+C=F\\right)\\]\n\\[(30,E,A)=\\text{def}B,C,D\\leftarrow(E+F=G,F+H=A,H+A=B\\right)\\]\n\\[(30,G,C)=\\text{def}C,(D+B)=G,(D+E)=D,G+F=G\\]\nthen $F=C+E=D+D=F+C=5$\nHence $A=F+D=C+4=H+4=D+8=A+H=5+5=10$\n\nThe value of $A+H$ in the eight-term sequence $A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H$ is $10$.",
